+## A build plan shows its code
+
+Every step in a build plan carries the code it will write, as a diff under
+`docs/plans/<feature>-code/`, shown by `make_page.py` plan mode (#469, #473). Each step opens with
+one plain sentence per change, then one captioned block of code per change, with line numbers and
+no mid-word wrapping; tests are folded under their names. The builder refuses a draft that breaks
+a readable-code rule (`scripts/readability.py`, the house standard in
+`rules/standards/coding-philosophy.md`), so the user never reads code that hasn't been made
+readable. The user ticks Looks right on each step; the copy box holds the approval only when every
+step is ticked, and an edit on the page comes back as changed lines that I apply to the draft.
+"Build plan approved" is refused until the page is recorded, linked in a reply, and built after
+the last draft edit (#470). In Build, each draft is the builder's specification, and the QA page
+lists every departure, compared line by line with spacing ignored (#471).
+
+**Why:** a build plan that only named files and line counts let unwanted habits reach a pull
+request before aj could see them; aj asked to review the code itself before it is written. The
+first drafts used names like `_rc` and wrapped mid-word, which aj could not read; aj chose a
+check that refuses unreadable code before they see it, because friction makes review easy to
+skip (aj, 2026-11-04, "Design approved: code preview and issue rule 1A 2A 3B 4A" and
+"Readable code approved: code preview 1A 2* 3A 4A").
 
